  "worked_solution": "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{2}{x}-5 \\\\ y=8x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x}-5 & =8x+1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{x} & =8x+6 \\\\ 2 & =8{x}^{2}+6x \\\\ 8{x}^{2}+6x-2 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+3x-1 & =0 \\\\ (4x-1)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{4},-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ and $x=-1.$ To find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the second equation.\n\n- Substituting $x=\\frac{1}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =8x+1 \\\\  & =8(\\frac{1}{4})+1 \\\\  & =2+1 \\\\  & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(\\frac{1}{4},3).$\n\n- Substituting $x=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =8x+1 \\\\  & =8(-1)+1 \\\\  & =-8+1 \\\\  & =-7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $(-1,-7).$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the points of intersection are $A(-1,-7)$ and $B(\\frac{1}{4},3).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/assets/5432e52f6099ec6e2d72ea6031e303eeaa0043129eea40baa064dd59b0d46520.png)",
